    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis explores the complex relationship between Irish nationalism and antisemitism through the lens of Michael Davitt's life and works. Although Ireland has been portrayed as relatively tolerant towards Jews, particularly when compared to other European countries, this thesis questions that narrative by examining instances of antisemitism within Irish society from the late 19th to early 20th centuries. Michael Davitt, a prominent Irish nationalist, offers a unique perspective on this issue. Despite his support for Jewish causes and opposition to antisemitic actions, Davitt was not immune to the prejudices of his time, as demonstrated by his views on events like the Dreyfus Affair and the Boer War. The thesis delves into Davitt’s early life, his education in England, and the experiences that shaped his broader humanitarianism and his specific stance towards the Jewish community in Ireland. By examining Davitt's public and private writings, this work sheds light on the contradictions in his views and the broader context of Irish antisemitism within the nationalist movement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fay, P. (2024). Michael Davitt and Antisemitism in ireland. Version 1. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gq601rq6222/version/1. https://doi.org/10.25740/gq601rq6222.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
